+A recursive type is walked as any other, so a tree is a tag inside a tag, and a field of type `json.Json` is written and
+read as itself. `json.encodeValue` writes a value as a `Json` value rather than text, to put in a larger one or to print
+with `json.printPretty`:
+
+```nanyx
+::dependency nanyx.json 0.3.0
+
+import nanyx.json/json as json
+
+type Tree = #leaf(int) | #node(left: Tree, right: Tree)
+
+type Event = (kind: string, tree: Tree, payload: json.Json)
+
+def event = Event(
+  kind = "grown"
+  tree = #node(left = #leaf(1), right = #leaf(2))
+  payload = #array([#bool(true), #null])
+)
+
+match json.encodeValue(event)
+  | #some(j) -> println(json.printPretty(j, indent = 2))
+  | failure -> println(json.describe(failure))
+
+match json.decode(
+  '{"kind": "cut", "tree": {"leaf": 7}, "payload": {"by": "Ada"}}'
+  into = type Event
+)
+  | #some(read) -> println("{read.kind}: {json.print(read.payload)}")
+  | failure -> println(json.describe(failure))
+
+-- {
+--   "kind": "grown",
+--   "tree": {
+--     "node": {
+--       "left": {
+--         "leaf": 1
+--       },
+--       "right": {
+--         "leaf": 2
+--       }
+--     }
+--   },
+--   "payload": [
+--     true,
+--     null
+--   ]
+-- }
+-- cut: {"by":"Ada"}
+```
+
+Until a compiler fix lands, `json.encode` can't write a nominal type, even one of the module that calls it
+([#2382](https://github.com/nanyx-lang/nanyx/issues/2382)), which `json.encodeWith(x, json.encoderFor(type T))` can.
+A set, and a map whose keys aren't strings, can't be read: read a list, and make one of it.
 
